1. `Maps` : maps is a data structure . maps are of four types maps , unordered maps,  multimaps, unordered multimaps. 
    maps are used to create different data types key value pairs . Maps store that key value pairs in sorted order 
    wherease unordered maps don't maintain that sorted order. Maps is not of continuous nature like arrays has , means
    its elements are exist in pair and they pair can be exist anywhere in the memory but they will be linked with each
    other we can't use i++ iterator logically but we have to still use this as c++ internally manage this . 
	1. `Syntax to declare a map` : we have to give two data types as input one for key(first) and for corresponding
	value(second) . `map <int,string> m;` this is the map of int keys corresponding string values .
		1. `Inserting values in map` : there are 2 to 3 ways to insert values in map.
			1. `first way` : `m[5] = "ARC";` now for 5 as key ARC as corresponding value is stored.
			2. `second way` : `m.insert({5,"ARC"});` 
			3. `third way` : `m.insert(make_pair(5,"ARC"));` and if we inserted any value for the existing key-value pair
			then for that key value will be overwrite.
		2. `accessing values from a map` : 
			1. `first through key` : if you know key and wanted to access corresponding value then `cout << m[5] << '\n';` .
			2. `second to print whole map key corresponding values ` : then create a iterator for map and then run from
			begin to end in loop and then print that `map<int,string> :: iterator it; for(it = m.begin() ; it != m.end()
			; it++){cout << (*it).first << ' ' << (*it).second << '\n';} ` 
			3. `third way modern iterator way` : `for(auto &pair : m){cout << pair.first << ' ' << pair.second << '\n';}`
			and in whatever order will will enter our key value pairs but map will automatically manage to sort our keys
			along with their respective pair. And for strings that will be in lexographical order.
		3. `m.size()` : this will return the size of our map the number of elements(key - value pair means key with
		corresponding value will be counted as one) present in m map will give .
		4. `time complexity ` : time complexity of calling `m[1];` is O(log(n)) means even if are not inserting the value
		this to be calling will consume O(log(n)) time complexity so call every key value pair carefully and also if we
		didn't inserted any value with corresponding key then our that value will be bare minimum means if int is the type
		of value then for leaving empty will store 0 by default and for string that will store a empty string . Means that
		if we run m[5] and didn't insert any value then 5 as key will be inserted in our map. Also keys of map are unique
		we don't insert duplicate keys in the map and if we tried to do this then our existing key's value will be replaced 
		with new value . And even time complexity of accessing any key - value pair will be O(log(n)) even if we just accessed
		key or even key-value pair O(log(n)) and so for accessing values using loop then time complexity of that loop will
		be n times O(n) that is `* O(n)` as we will access n key-value pairs . And for inserting n values time complexity
		will be `n * O(log(n))` . And time complexity even depends on the data type we are inserting suppose if our key
		is of string then our time complexity will be s.size()* O(log(n)) here s will be size of the string we are inserting
		as a key and n is the size of our map . Reason behind this is maps uses a self balancing tree red black tree and
		in this our map will internally first check whether that key exist or not if exist then at which place this will
		be placed because map sort key-value pairs also and for string this has to check lexographical order also which
		will take little more time and this size won't matter for small strings but for bigger this will matter a lot  .
		
	2. `Some useful functions of maps` : 
		1. `m.find(5)` : we can use this find on keys only and this is iterator type this will return iterator if found
		5 key in map then this will point on that otherwise this will point to m.end() and we can't use this on values .
		This `auto it = m.find(5); ` time complexity is O(log(n)). As generally whichever self balance trees we are using
		like maps use red black self balancing trees then its time complexity for accessing , insertion and finding etc
		basic function is O(log(n)). And even if there is no value attached to that key then our find will point to m.end().
			1. `ex` : `auto it = m.find(5); if(it == m.end()){cout << "NOT FOUND!" << '\n'; else{cout << (*it).first << ' '
			<< (*it).second << '\n';` 
		2. `m.erase()` : this can used for both types of input like direct value or iterator pointing on that place 
			1. `putting direct values` : `m.erase(5);`  then this will erase key-value pair which having 5 as a key. But
			if we gave any key which doesn't exist in our map then this won't throw error.
			2. `putting iterator as input` : `auto it = m.find(5); m.erase(it);`  then this will erase both key-value pairs
			.But if we gave any key which doesn't exist in our map then this will throw a segmentation fault error. So to
			avoid such errors we can first check whether that key - value pair exist in our map or not and then use `ex:` `
			auto it = m.find(5) ; if(it != m.end()){m.erase(it);}`
		3. `clearing map` : `m.clear();` this will clear our map m means make our m map as empty .
		
		
2. `.clear()` : this is standard function and this clears almost any data type , container or even datastructure empty 
    means , even string or map . `ex: s.clear();` then our s string will get empty string. 
    
    
3. `.size()` : this is standard function and this gives the size of  data type , number of elements of a  container or
    even number of elements present in  datastructure  means , even string or map . `ex: m.size()` then this would return
    number of key-value pairs present in m map . 
    
    
4. `.empty()` : this is standard functions and this works on all data types , containers , data structures and check
    whether that they are empty or not and this works on boolean `ex: if(s.empty() == true){cout << "YES" << '\n';} 
    else{cout << "NO" << '\n';} ` .
    
    
5. `clarification on why .clear , .size and .empty wont work on whom` : Just like `.clear()` and `.size()`, 
    **`empty()` does not work on primitive data types** (like `int`, `float`, `char`, or raw arrays like 
    `int arr[]`). You cannot write `int x; x.empty();` because primitive types do not have member functions.
